WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:07.311
If you remember back to the last caesium video,
Pete and Neil tried to launch a bit of caesium

00:00:07.311 --> 00:00:14.440
metal into water with the small metallic collider
and they had a few problems with surface area

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:24.150
and caesium still being stuck in the glass
as well.

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:30.310
What I have been asked to do today, is take
this ampoule of caesium into this piece of

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:35.920
machinery here which is one of our glove boxes,
try and get it out of the glass so that we

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:40.789
can put it in as is.

00:00:40.789 --> 00:00:47.120
Caesium has quite a surprising use, it is
used to measure time. I don’t know if you

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:55.690
have ever thought about it but you could define
one second as a fraction of a day, one sixtieth

00:00:55.690 --> 00:01:00.760
of a minute which is which is a sixtieth of
an hour which is then a twenty fourth of a

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:06.760
day. But the trouble with that is that you
will never be able to tell if the earth was

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:12.040
going slower or speeding up because the length
of the day would change and you would not

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:18.670
notice it so scientists have to have an independent
way of defining the second and this is where

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:20.080
caesium comes in.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:26.530
And so now the caesium is going in as well.
Alright, so we will shut the port door

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:36.250
So the way in which scientists define the
second is in terms of microwaves and the frequency

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:43.390
of the microwaves, we need a standard, something
that can define a radiowave very very precisely,

00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:46.810
and this is where caesium comes in.

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:49.990
So what I need to do now is open up this door
here.

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:56.409
The point about caesium is that it is a big
atom it can absorb microwaves, the absorption

00:01:56.409 --> 00:02:03.890
of the microwave causes an electron to move
from one energy level to another and the advantage

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:10.330
of caesium over all other materials is that
this frequency is enormously precise.

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:18.989
It is pretty cool it is really good, it is
sort of a really pale gold colour. It is still

00:02:18.989 --> 00:02:19.989
molten at the moment.

00:02:19.989 --> 00:02:24.959
It is rather like tuning a radio, I don’t
know if you’ve ever tuned a radio but as

00:02:24.959 --> 00:02:32.019
you turn the knob, sometimes, you don’t
have to be very precise , you can put it almost

00:02:32.019 --> 00:02:36.659
anywhere and you get your favourite station,
but there are some stations where you have

00:02:36.659 --> 00:02:42.420
to adjust it very very accurately and there
is just one tiny place where you can get the

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:51.670
right radio station and caesium is like that,
it defines the frequency very very accurately,

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:57.560
if you don’t hit it right it does not absorb
the radiowaves and so you can use it as a

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:05.790
standard, so make sure so make sure that everybody
has exactly the same frequency of their microwave

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:10.749
and then once everybody has got the same,
then it is quite easy for everybody to agree

00:03:10.749 --> 00:03:14.319
that they will count a certain number of waves
as being a second.

00:03:14.319 --> 00:03:19.219
We are going to store the caesium under oil
so that it does not react with air when it

00:03:19.219 --> 00:03:23.870
comes out. So this is ready now for Pete to
chuck it in some water and we will stand very

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:28.909
far back when he does it.

00:03:28.909 --> 00:03:32.609
So we have got caesium, we have taken it out
of the ampoule, Neil did that the other day

00:03:32.609 --> 00:03:37.069
with Debbie in their glove box, they have
put it into a small plastic weighing boat

00:03:37.069 --> 00:03:41.689
and immersed it in oil so it can’t react
with air . Now what we need to do is a very

00:03:41.689 --> 00:03:48.069
technical operation is to punch a hole into
the plastic boat so that we can lift it out

00:03:48.069 --> 00:03:52.109
with a piece of string so we can drop it into
some water.

00:03:52.109 --> 00:03:57.559
I’ve got my friend, Dr Red.

00:03:57.559 --> 00:04:00.889
It is always good to be very very careful
when you are doing chemistry like this, just

00:04:00.889 --> 00:04:06.370
in case it goes wrong we have the safety procedures
in place for us, in case we get surprised.

00:04:06.370 --> 00:04:11.680
So now Neil is going to pass a piece of string
through the hole so that we can put it at

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:21.459
the end of a pretty long fishing line so that
we can drop it into water.

00:04:21.459 --> 00:04:27.520
So this is Neil’s new device, it’s a very
very fancy erection. Essentially we have used

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:34.550
a laboratory stand, and we have an eyelet
so this is just to act as a point where we

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:39.480
can raise up our caesium on a piece of string.
At the moment, the caesium you can see is

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:44.120
a lovely gold colour. Would you believe me,
it’s melting.

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:48.700
Look at it. Oh yeah it’s very warm here
today and the caesium’s melted. So we are

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:51.910
just going to try and freeze it again.

00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:59.050
Well we are going to try and refreeze the
caesium by cooling it with liquid nitrogen.

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:00.270
Ok everybody be careful.

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:07.160
So we have our caesium under oil. We now need
to lift it up using the new device, and then

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:12.350
drop it in the water, and let’s see what
kind of chemical reaction it does today.

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:21.110
Everybody ready? Neil?

00:05:21.110 --> 00:05:25.470
Yep!

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:30.670
Wow, quite a spectacular reaction. As the
caesium reacted with the water, gave away

00:05:30.670 --> 00:05:34.150
its electron, off she goes, it’s caesium
plus.

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:35.950
Awesome, it was pretty good.

00:05:35.950 --> 00:05:39.310
Yeah, it’s pretty great you know when, when
Debbie and Neil took the caesium out of the

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:42.640
ampoule they were really really good for us
because they gave us a really large surface

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:46.430
area, so it could react quickly as soon as
it went in the water. Generating all that

00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:50.280
lovely hydrogen ready to explode and make
a bang. Great, good chemistry.

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:59.510
Well, the reason that caesium reacts so violently
is that it is a very large atom, so an electron

00:05:59.510 --> 00:06:08.120
can be removed very easily, in fact if you
dissolve caesium in water, on a very short

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:14.370
time scale, a millionth of a second or so,
it dissociates into caesium-plus and an electron,

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:21.190
and the energy of the electron interacting
with the water is enough to separate the two

00:06:21.190 --> 00:06:29.640
and once the electron is in the water, it
then reacts with the water so that H2O dissociates

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:39.240
into a hydrogen atom and OH-minus, so in fact
what you are making is a caesium salt which

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:46.580
is rather like caustic soda, it’s a strong
alkali and hydrogen, and it is the rapid formation

00:06:46.580 --> 00:06:53.460
of hydrogen gas that causes part of the shock
of the reaction and then the hydrogen will

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:58.530
usually catch fire and react with the oxygen
in the air to give you a small explosion or

00:06:58.530 --> 00:07:01.090
a big one depending how much caesium you’ve
used.

